WEBVTT

00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:03.355
The problem: a small study group wants to discuss

00:00:03.398 --> 00:00:06.796
an assignment, but the conversation gets delayed by app

00:00:07.011 --> 00:00:11.269
installations, account creation, and confusing meeting links.

00:00:11.441 --> 00:00:13.419
People should be able to open a link and

00:00:13.591 --> 00:00:15.226
start working together.

00:00:15.376 --> 00:00:18.581
The solution is Meet Hub: a browser meeting room

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:20.301
for up to four people.

00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:23.484
Create a room, share one link, check your camera

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.720
and microphone, and join.

00:00:25.935 --> 00:00:29.849
Audio, video, screen sharing, and chat stay inside the

00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:31.312
same application.

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:34.925
This is Meet Hub, an end-term software project by

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.600
Anuj Kumar Soni.

00:00:36.645 --> 00:00:40.000
The interface is built with React and Vite.

00:00:40.043 --> 00:00:44.559
The project's own Node and WebSocket backend coordinates membership,

00:00:44.731 --> 00:00:50.237
chat, and media negotiation, while native WebRTC transports media.

00:00:50.323 --> 00:00:53.505
Render provides a hosting option for this code.

00:00:53.591 --> 00:00:56.860
The four-person limit keeps the peer mesh appropriate for

00:00:56.946 --> 00:00:58.667
small study groups.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.914
The repository includes setup instructions, architecture decisions, tests, and

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.720
clear limitations.

00:01:05.806 --> 00:01:08.559
Meet Hub turns a shared link into a working

00:01:08.946 --> 00:01:09.505
conversation.

00:01:09.505 --> 00:01:12.996
This is the real Meet Hub meeting, recorded with

00:01:12.996 --> 00:01:15.711
a guest joining through the shared link.

00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:18.426
Both video tiles come from actual cameras.

00:01:18.426 --> 00:01:21.917
The room shows who is present and keeps the

00:01:21.917 --> 00:01:23.468
main controls within reach.

00:01:23.468 --> 00:01:26.959
This is the experience the project is designed around:

00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:30.449
a small group getting into a conversation quickly, inside

00:01:30.449 --> 00:01:31.613
one browser application.

00:01:31.613 --> 00:01:33.935
Let us start with the first workflow.

00:01:33.978 --> 00:01:37.204
A meeting name creates a unique room address.

00:01:37.376 --> 00:01:40.559
Before joining, the prejoin screen asks for a display

00:01:40.645 --> 00:01:43.799
name and lets the participant check devices.

00:01:43.857 --> 00:01:46.495
The preview shows the real camera feed.

00:01:46.538 --> 00:01:48.989
A participant can also choose to join with the

00:01:49.204 --> 00:01:52.516
camera and microphone off, then enable them during the

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.247
call.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.215
Clear permission messages explain what to do if a

00:01:56.473 --> 00:01:58.241
browser blocks a device.

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:00.172
Next, invite someone.

00:02:00.215 --> 00:02:03.527
The invitation dialog copies the room link and confirms

00:02:03.613 --> 00:02:04.860
that it was copied.

00:02:04.903 --> 00:02:07.914
Opening that link in another browser session goes straight

00:02:07.957 --> 00:02:10.266
to the same room's join screen.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.849
After joining, participants discover each other through the project's

00:02:13.935 --> 00:02:15.627
own signaling server.

00:02:15.685 --> 00:02:18.710
Their browsers then negotiate the media connection.

00:02:18.753 --> 00:02:22.108
The server handles the room; the browsers exchange the

00:02:22.194 --> 00:02:23.925
actual audio and video.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.624
Now the conversation can become useful.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.849
Room chat lets participants exchange notes while the call

00:02:30.065 --> 00:02:30.925
continues.

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:34.667
The server assigns the sender's identity, so clients cannot

00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:37.806
simply claim another participant sent a message.

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:41.763
Text that looks like HTML remains ordinary text.

00:02:41.806 --> 00:02:44.731
The participant panel shows who is present.

00:02:44.903 --> 00:02:48.430
Raised hands communicate a request to speak, while microphone

00:02:48.516 --> 00:02:51.441
and camera controls update the media tracks and the

00:02:51.527 --> 00:02:53.602
status other participants see.

00:02:53.677 --> 00:02:56.860
For discussing an assignment, screen sharing is often the

00:02:56.989 --> 00:02:58.524
most important feature.

00:02:58.552 --> 00:03:01.935
The participant chooses a capture source through the browser.

00:03:02.065 --> 00:03:04.946
Meet Hub replaces the outgoing video track with that

00:03:05.032 --> 00:03:06.151
screen track.

00:03:06.237 --> 00:03:09.161
Stopping the share restores the camera without leaving the

00:03:09.247 --> 00:03:11.957
room or generating a new invitation.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.240
Sharing also works while the camera is switched off,

00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:17.945
subject to the browser's screen-capture support.

00:03:18.012 --> 00:03:21.333
A complete application must also handle failure.

00:03:21.376 --> 00:03:24.903
a network interruption changes the connection state and displays

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:26.452
an actionable message.

00:03:26.538 --> 00:03:30.667
When connectivity returns, Meet Hub reconnects the room and

00:03:30.839 --> 00:03:32.559
restores collaboration.

00:03:32.903 --> 00:03:35.828
Sending a new chat message confirms that the room

00:03:35.914 --> 00:03:37.290
connection works again.

00:03:37.978 --> 00:03:43.140
Join timeouts, heartbeat checks, reconnect backoff, and stale negotiation

00:03:43.226 --> 00:03:45.161
handling support recovery.

00:03:45.462 --> 00:03:48.559
An optional TURN relay helps when a direct media

00:03:48.645 --> 00:03:50.796
connection is blocked by the network.

00:03:52.258 --> 00:03:55.613
Leaving the call stops the participant's capture tracks and

00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:57.161
updates room membership.

00:03:57.677 --> 00:04:00.688
The user returns to Meet Hub, where recent room

00:04:00.774 --> 00:04:02.753
links are stored in that browser.

00:04:03.355 --> 00:04:06.538
Room chat is temporary and bounded to the latest

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:08.000
fifty messages.

00:04:08.258 --> 00:04:11.527
The Node backend clears its in-memory room state when

00:04:11.613 --> 00:04:14.581
the room empties or the server restarts.

00:04:14.882 --> 00:04:17.806
Meet Hub does not record calls or provide a

00:04:17.892 --> 00:04:19.613
permanent message archive.
